  "Question": "Let \\(f\\) be a function for which \\(f\\!\\left(\\dfrac{x}{3}\\right) = x^2 + x + 1.\\) Find the sum of all values of \\(z\\) for which \\(f(3z) = 7.\\)",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(-\\dfrac{1}{3}\\)",
    "B": "\\(-\\dfrac{1}{9}\\)",
    "C": "\\(0\\)",
    "D": "\\(\\dfrac{5}{9}\\)",
    "E": "\\(\\dfrac{5}{3}\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "B",
  "Solution": "To use the given rule for \\(f(3z)\\), its input \\(x/3\\) must equal \\(3z\\). Thus \\(x=9z\\), or \\(z=x/9\\). It is simpler to find the corresponding values of \\(x\\) first.\n\nThe given output equals \\(7\\) exactly when\n\\[\nx^2+x+1=7,\n\\]\nwhich is equivalent to\n\\[\nx^2+x-6=(x+3)(x-2)=0.\n\\]\nA product is zero exactly when at least one factor is zero, so the only possibilities are \\(x=-3\\) and \\(x=2\\). Therefore\n\\[\nz=-\\frac13\\quad\\text{or}\\quad z=\\frac29.\n\\]\nEach value corresponds to a root of the original output equation, and the relation \\(x=9z\\) is reversible, so these are all the solutions. Their sum is\n\\[\n-\\frac13+\\frac29=\\boxed{-\\frac19},\n\\]\nchoice B.",
